; A `.cbor`-over-a-REFERENCE as a TYPE-CHOICE ARM (`bytes .cbor bar / tstr`) GENERATES: the
; reference is a dependency like any other, so the rule graph orders `bar` before `a` and the arm
; resolves it as a registered type rather than as a dangling forward reference.
;
; That ordering is what this fixture pins. A control operator's right-hand side names types
; (`bytes .cbor bar` depends on `bar` exactly as `[bar]` does), so `dep_graph`'s reference walk
; covers `type1.operator` as well as `type1.type2`. Without that edge the target can be ordered
; AFTER its user, and the arm then holds a bare `Rust` ident naming no registered struct — which
; panics every downstream lookup that assumes one (`EnumVariant::group_ctor_record_fields`,
; `IntermediateTypes::is_enum`). The choice-arm and the plain-member spellings
; (`a = [f: bytes .cbor bar]`) both went through that one hole, and one edge covers both.
;
; The target being an ALIAS (`bar = uint`, a transparent `pub type Bar = u64;`) is deliberately part
; of the shape: it is the case where the target's structural type is NOT a struct, so it exercises
; exactly the lookups the missing edge used to break. The arm keeps the `Alias` node the target
; resolves to, so the variant is typed `Bar` and an annotated target's codec routes through it.
; The inline-target form (`bytes .cbor uint`) never needed an edge and has always generated. The sibling spelling puts such a target under a top-level rule
; BODY (tests/robustness/cbor_ref_rule_body.cddl); it also generates, but reaches its failure
; through a different site and needs the parse-side half of the fix as well as this edge — so it
; keeps its own row rather than folding into this one. Chain DEPTH is covered by
; tests/robustness/cbor_ref_alias_chain.cddl.
a = bytes .cbor bar / tstr
bar = uint
